<section>
  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p>
    A particle moves on a straight line with <i>v</i>(<i>t</i>) = 3 + 2<i>t</i> − <i>t</i>²,
    where <i>v</i> is in m/s and <i>t</i> is in seconds. The displacement
    <i>s</i> is zero when <i>t</i> = 0. Find expressions for
    <i>a</i>(<i>t</i>) and <i>s</i>(<i>t</i>), and find <i>v</i>, <i>a</i>
    and <i>s</i> when <i>t</i> = 1 s.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><i>a</i>(<i>t</i>) = 2 − 2<i>t</i></li>
    <li><i>s</i>(<i>t</i>) = 3<i>t</i> + <i>t</i>² − <i>t</i>³/3</li>
    <li>When <i>t</i> = 1: <i>v</i> = 4 m/s, <i>a</i> = 0, <i>s</i> = 3 + 1 − ⅓ = 3.67 m</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Show that the particle is instantaneously at rest when <i>t</i> = 3 s,
    and find its displacement then.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><i>v</i>(3) = 3 + 6 − 9 = 0, so it is instantaneously at rest</li>
    <li><i>a</i>(3) = 2 − 6 = −4 m/s², so it then reverses</li>
    <li><i>s</i>(3) = 9 + 9 − 9 = 9 m</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    If the <i>t</i>² term were missing, the acceleration would be the
    constant 2 m/s² and <i>s</i> = 3<i>t</i> + <i>t</i>².
  </p>
</section>
